     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Las locuciones condicionales de salvedad o excepción ('a menos que', 'a no ser que', 'salvo que', 'excepto que') introducen la única circunstancia que impediría el cumplimiento de la cláusula principal. Salvo en construcciones con indicativo restringidas a hechos ya constatados con 'salvo que' ('no lo creo, salvo que tú digas lo contrario'), la norma culta prescribe siempre el subjuntivo."
+      "content": "You know *a menos que* from B1 Unit 21, *a no ser que* and *salvo que* from B2 Unit 9, and *excepto que* and *excepto si* from B2 Unit 11. Each one names the only thing that could stop the main clause from being true: *Iremos al campo a menos que llueva*. A useful test is to swap the phrase for *si no*: *Iremos al campo si no llueve* means the same.\n\nThe mood depends on the phrase. *A menos que*, *a no ser que*, *salvo que* and *excepto que* take the subjunctive, because the exception has not happened yet. *Salvo si* and *excepto si* are *si* phrases and take the indicative. After a past or conditional verb, the subjunctive is the imperfect."
